{
  "name": "How to Handle Emergency Interior Storm Damage",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Dealing with emergency interior storm damage can feel like a whirlwind, but breaking it down into steps makes it more manageable. Here’s a process that usually works best:",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2",
      "text": "**Step 1: Secure the Area and Document.** As soon as it's safe, your first move is to make the area secure. This might mean shutting off utilities if there’s a risk, but only if you know how to do it safely. Then, grab your phone or camera and thoroughly document all the damage with photos and videos. Don't touch anything yet; just get clear evidence for your insurance company.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3",
      "text": "**Step 2: Prevent Further Loss.** This is where temporary mitigation comes in. Think tarps over a damaged roof, boarding up broken windows, or using a wet vac to remove standing water. The goal here is to stop the problem from escalating. This isn’t the permanent fix, just a hold-over.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4",
      "text": "**Step 3: Contact Your Insurer and a Professional.** As soon as these first two steps are handled, reach out to your insurance company. Many policies require timely reporting, often within 24-48 hours. Then, it's smart to call in a qualified professional. Especially if there's significant water, structural concerns, or potential electrical issues, you'll want someone experienced. Here at HH Remodeling LLC, we’re ready to assist with emergency storm damage repair in areas like Aurora and surrounding Denver communities.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
